1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,159
Two identical lamps are connected in series in one circuit, and in parallel in another.

2
00:00:05,159 --> 00:00:11,079
In each circuit, one lamp is unscrewed. Predict: what will happen to the other lamp in each circuit?

3
00:00:11,079 --> 00:00:12,076
Pause and decide.

4
00:00:13,135 --> 00:00:17,297
Watch. In the series circuit, removing one lamp makes the other go out.

5
00:00:18,355 --> 00:00:23,338
In the parallel circuit, removing one lamp leaves the other shining just as brightly.

6
00:00:24,397 --> 00:00:30,493
Explain what you saw. In series there is only one path, so a gap anywhere stops the current everywhere.

7
00:00:30,493 --> 00:00:34,421
In parallel, each lamp is on its own branch with the full voltage,

8
00:00:34,421 --> 00:00:37,469
so the other branch still forms a complete circuit.

9
00:00:37,469 --> 00:00:41,749
That is why the lights in a house can be switched on and off separately.

10
00:00:41,749 --> 00:00:44,035
Pause and explain it in your own words.

11
00:00:45,094 --> 00:00:50,076
In a parallel circuit, components sit on separate branches, like lanes on a highway.

12
00:00:50,076 --> 00:00:55,177
Each branch gets the full voltage, so adding identical lamps doesn't make them dimmer.

13
00:00:55,177 --> 00:00:59,866
If one lamp fails, the others stay on, which is why homes are wired in parallel.

14
00:01:00,925 --> 00:01:05,849
The total current is the sum of the branch currents: three 1-amp lamps draw 3 amps.

15
00:01:05,849 --> 00:01:10,187
Road analogies help us picture circuits, but every model has limitations.

16
00:01:10,187 --> 00:01:14,994
As scientific understanding grows, scientists develop better models and analogies.
